Feasibility — Quick Summary

Technically Feasible

Multirotor + RGB + thermal + GPS (standard, proven hardware)
Pixhawk/ArduPilot or PX4 (mature autopilot stack)
Basic human/animal/unknown AI classification (YOLO-class model on Jetson/Pi)
GPS-tagged alert dashboard

Needs Real Testing

Acoustic gunshot detection (~57% accuracy in cited research — not reliable alone)
Multi-angle occlusion handling (works in theory, unproven in field)
Threat-scoring tuning (needs real data to avoid false alerts)

Not Feasible at Prototype Stage

Full sanctuary-wide coverage
Range-level multi-drone fleet
Battery-swapping infrastructure
Solar forward stations

Operationally

✅ Integrates with existing Forest Dept hierarchy (Circle→Division→Range→Round→Beat)
✅ Positioned as augmenting guards, not replacing them
⚠️ Needs ongoing maintenance, training, battery logistics
⚠️ Remote-area connectivity is unreliable — needs local processing

Cost

✅ Single prototype drone: affordable for student/SIH build
❌ Multi-Range deployment + infrastructure: needs government-scale funding

Regulatory

⚠️ Needs DGCA drone clearance
⚠️ Border-adjacent sanctuaries (e.g., Kachchh/BSF zone) need extra security clearance
✅ Avoiding facial recognition = more privacy-feasible

Timeline (SIH-realistic)

Phase 1–2 (detection + acoustic + fusion): buildable and demoable
Phase 3 (threat scoring + patrol check): feasible with simulated data
Phase 4 (full dashboard + tethered UAV + fleet): simulate, don't build
